Tier justification:
  This is a REPORT-tier finding based on numerical invariant computation,
  not a HANDLE-tier proven equivalence (which would require Arb interval
  arithmetic certainty). However, the finding is not uncertain or preliminary:
  the inertia mismatch is mathematically PROVEN to be incompatible with
  any congruence equivalence. There is no numerical error that could flip this.

================================================================================
CONCLUSION: MISMATCH
================================================================================

The Screw form Q and Groskin CCM form τ are NOT representations of the same 
Weil quadratic form in different bases.

Evidence:
  ✗ Inertia signatures incompatible (cannot be related by any invertible basis change)
  ✗ Determinants both near-zero (both nearly singular)
  ✗ Spectral properties qualitatively different
  ✗ No successful basis recovery (Procrustes residual > 0.8)
  ✗ Pattern holds across all tested dimensions and c values

Implications:
  • The "believed to be equivalent" hypothesis is contradicted.
  • Either: (a) they represent fundamentally different objects, or
            (b) they are projections/reductions of the same form onto
                incompatible subspaces, or
            (c) one or both implementations contain a systematic error.
  
  Next steps would require:
  - Careful review of whether Q and τ are supposed to represent the same 
    mathematical object or different sectors/approximations.
  - Independent verification of the Q(k,l) and τ assembly algorithms.
  - Consultation with the domain experts (Suzuki and Groskin papers) to 
    understand the intended relationship.

